    "summary": "Epic Other One suite, tight jams and soaring guitar across a high‑energy set",
    "blurb": "The Frankfurt show delivers an electrifying second‑set jam, highlighted by a 36‑minute The Other One suite that flows into Truckin' and Comes A Time. Jerry’s shredding leads, Phil lays down thematic bass lines, and the rhythm section drives with tight, propulsive drumming.",
    "review": "On April 26, 1972 at Frankfurt’s Jahrhundert Halle the Dead unleashed one of their most memorable Europe ’72 performances. The first set rolls through a solid mix of roots rock and psychedelia—Bertha, China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider, Jack Straw and a rousing Good Lovin'—but it’s the second set that cements this night in legend. A monster Truckin' → Drums → The Other One sequence launches into an almost 36‑minute improvisational odyssey that never loses its momentum, eventually spilling over into Sugar Magnolia and Turn On Your Lovelight before the band caps the night with Goin’ Down The Road Feeling Bad and One More Saturday Night.\n\nJerry Garcia is “shredding” and “weaving magic” throughout, his lead on The Other One and Good Lovin' described as pure fire. Phil Lesh supplies the thematic backbone, spawning melodic motifs that echo the band’s early Clementine jams while keeping a solid groove despite occasional low‑mix bass. Bob Weir rocks hard on Sugar Magnolia and adds a sturdy rhythm layer. The drummers lock in with tight, propulsive playing—especially during the sprawling Other One suite—giving the whole performance a relentless drive. In short, this show captures the Dead at a creative peak, delivering high‑energy rock, exploratory jams, and moments of sheer musical joy that make it a must‑listen for any Deadhead.",
